# {{project.title}}

Static site built with Astro and Tailwind CSS, deployed to S3 and CloudFront.

## Local development

```
pnpm install
pnpm dev
```

## Build

```
pnpm build
```

`pnpm build` runs `astro build` and then `scripts/seo-check.ts`. The check is part of the build,
not a separate step, so a failure aborts before the CDK bundling step can pick up the output.

## How this site is structured

**`src/seo/routes.ts` is the single source of truth for every URL.** Titles, meta descriptions,
the primary navigation and the sitemap all read from it. A page declares which route it is and
`BaseLayout` supplies the rest. There is deliberately no `title` prop, because the failure this
prevents is a site shipping hundreds of pages that all carry the same template-default title.

Adding a page means adding it to that array. A page whose path is not registered throws at build.

**`src/styles/global.css` is the single styling source.** The `@theme` block closes the palette
and the breakpoint set with `--color-*: initial` and `--breakpoint-*: initial`, so a colour or
breakpoint that is not defined there does not exist. Element defaults (type scale, link colour,
focus ring, reduced motion) live in `@layer base`, which is why no heading, paragraph or link in
this site carries a class.

**Arbitrary Tailwind values are not allowed in `src/pages`.** `p-[13px]` belongs in a component or
in the `@theme` block, so a one-off measurement is defined once for the whole site instead of
being typed into a template. The build fails otherwise.

## What the build asserts

`scripts/seo-check.ts` fails the build on any of the following:

- a page with no title, or a title still carrying scaffold text
- two pages sharing a title or a meta description
- a missing or foreign-origin canonical link
- a page whose `<h1>` is empty in the static HTML, meaning a crawler never sees the heading
- a sitemap whose URL count disagrees with the registry
- a 404 page missing its heading or its noindex tag
- a class with no matching rule in the emitted CSS, which is what an off-palette typo produces
- a full-width button that never returns to auto width at a larger breakpoint
- an arbitrary Tailwind value inside `src/pages`
- emitted CSS over the size budget

## Deployment

Bitbucket Pipelines deploys staging automatically on `main` and production on a manual trigger.

{% if stagingDomain %}Point the staging domain after the first successful deploy:

```
pnpx @soliantconsulting/sld-dns-control-client point-staging-domain
```

That command finds the stack output whose key ends with `DistributionDomainName`. Do not rename
that output.
{% endif %}

Production needs `certificateArn` and `domainName` filled in at `cdk/src/env-production.ts`.

### What the CloudFront stack does differently from the React SPA starter

- An unknown URL returns a real **404** carrying `/404.html`, rather than the homepage at HTTP 200.
  403 is mapped the same way, because S3 behind Origin Access Control answers a missing key with
  AccessDenied rather than NoSuchKey.
- A viewer-request function resolves directory URLs (`/about/` to `/about/index.html`), which S3
  does not do on its own.
- Only hashed assets under `/_astro/` are marked immutable. Documents revalidate.
- Retired pages are **pruned** from the bucket, so a page removed from the site stops being served.
  The build refuses to hand over an empty or partial output, which is what makes pruning safe.
- Each deploy issues exactly one `/*` invalidation rather than one per file.
